{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Jacob came whole.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 79:5) — whole in his body, whole in his wealth, whole in his Torah. There is an allusion to the matter: שָׁלֵם is an acronym for שְׁאֵר לִמּוּד מָזוֹן. For שְׁאֵר means his body, for by the fact that Jacob was left alone, he departed from the category of equivalence as though something were lacking from his wealth — for one who is left alone appears to the world as lacking a pocket. And afterward, when he said, ״כִּי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11), he set the matter right and acknowledged that he is whole, lacking nothing, and that he has everything — this is the meaning of whole in his wealth. And whole in his Torah: for when it says, ״וַתֵּקַע כַּף יֶרֶךְ יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 32:26), it was hinted to him that he has no hand or portion in the secrets of Torah, as explained above, and by setting this right through saying ״וְכִי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״, he became whole in his Torah as well — for this depends on that, as explained above at the verse ״וַיִּוָּתֵר יַעֲקֹב לְבַדּוֹ״ (Genesis 32:25). And whole in his body — from the limp of the thigh, for when he came to the city of Shechem he was healed."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The acronym שָׁלֵם = שְׁאֵר לִמּוּד מָזוֹן ('flesh, learning, sustenance') is rendered letter-for-letter; the phrase יָצָא מִגֶּדֶר הַשִּׁוּוּי ('departed from the category of equivalence/parity') likely refers to being alone without match or equal company, translated literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:57.648448573Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}